==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|monstreux}}, from {{der|en|la|monstrosus|mōnstrōsus}}. Compare {{m|en|monstruous}}.

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[hideous|Hideous]] or [[frightful]].
#* Shakespeare
#*: So bad a death argues a '''monstrous''' life.
# [[enormously|Enormously]] [[large]].
#: ''a '''monstrous''' height''
#: ''a '''monstrous''' ox''
# [[freakish|Freakish]] or [[grotesque]].
#* John Locke
#*: a '''monstrous''' birth
#* Jeremy Taylor
#*: He, therefore, that refuses to do good to them whom he is bound to love {{...}} is unnatural and '''monstrous''' in his affections.
# Of, or relating to a [[mythical]] [[monster]]; full of monsters.
#* Milton
#*: Where thou, perhaps, under the whelming tide / Visitest the bottom of the '''monstrous''' world.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[marvellous|Marvellous]]; [[strange]].

====Synonyms====
* See also [[Wikisaurus:gigantic]]

